cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this code or the results in it, please cite the paper below.
title: >-
  Who Leaves Matters More Than How Many: The Utility Cost of Revocable Patient
  Consent in Cross-Silo Federated Learning
abstract: >-
  Blockchain consent layers for federated healthcare machine learning let
  patients revoke participation at any time, but the utility cost of that
  capability has not been measured. This work quantifies it on 30-day
  readmission prediction (UCI Diabetes 130-US hospitals, 101,766 encounters,
  K=3 silos, 30 seeds), decomposing consent churn into three physically distinct
  regimes. A count-matched isolation test, paired by seed so that both arms
  share a partition and remove an identical number of patients, shows that
  removing an entire positive-heavy silo costs 0.053 AUROC against 0.015 for the
  same headcount drawn at random: a paired difference of -0.038 (95% CI -0.055
  to -0.021, p = 0.0001). The damage is carried by who leaves rather than how
  many leave. Rerunning the full grid with a higher-capacity MLP client
  reproduces every delta within one standard deviation, so the cost structure is
  model-independent.
authors:
  - family-names: Nhan
    given-names: Thuan
    affiliation: "Middle Tennessee State University"
  - family-names: Poudel
